$\mathrm{Pr}, \mathrm{Nd}, \mathrm{Tb}$ and Dy exhibit +4 state in their oxides as they acquire configurations closer to stable $4 \mathrm{f}^{\circ}$ and $4 \mathrm{f}^7$ configurations.

\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{Pr}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^1, \mathrm{Nd}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^2, \mathrm{Pm}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^3 \\
& \mathrm{Sm}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^4, \mathrm{Eu}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^5, \mathrm{Gd}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^6, \\
& \mathrm{~Tb}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^7, \mathrm{Dy}^{4+}=[\mathrm{Xe}] 4 \mathrm{f}^6 .
\end{aligned}

In the periodic table of elements, gallium (Ga) is grouped in the boron family (group 13) which include the semi-metal boron (B) and the metals aluminium ( $\mathrm{Al}$ ), gallium ( $\mathrm{Ga}$ ), indium (In) and thalium ( $\mathrm{Tl})$.

